WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:15.269
China has starred in what has been the greatest
economic revolution in the history of mankind.

00:00:15.269 --> 00:00:21.430
However, in spite of this, in 2017, both Moody's
and Standard and Poor's, 2 of the 3 main rating

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:26.830
agencies, reduced the Chinese debt’s rating.

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:36.010
For its part, the International Monetary Fund
itself has been issuing warnings on this for

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:37.290
months...

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:41.230
Yes, the Chinese economy is growing strongly,
but... that doesn’t mean that everything

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:43.460
is going well... not at all.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:47.470
In recent years, - as we’ve already discussed
in a video I will leave at the end with a

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:54.969
description – China's economy has seen one
parameter soar exponentially: corporate debt.

00:00:54.969 --> 00:00:56.410
And this isn’t small news…

00:00:56.410 --> 00:01:01.090
Chinese corporate debt accounts for almost
170% of China's GDP.

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:08.240
To give you an idea of what this means, we
could say that this 170% GDP level is one

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:17.740
of the highest on the planet... and is, for
example, twice that of the United States.

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:21.960
But...

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:26.430
some of you may be wondering... if the Chinese
economy seems to be doing so well...

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:29.460
How did its companies accumulate such a huge
debt?

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:34.070
Well... the truth is that in recent years
Chinese companies have gone on a kind of spending

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:35.430
spree all over the world...

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:37.450
they have bought everything...

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:48.200
foreign companies, ports, airports, mines,
railways... even soccer teams.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:53.290
The Chinese "invasion" seemed unstoppable...
and that’s precisely what caused so many

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:58.250
countries to develop an aggressive discourse
against this kind of occupation...

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:02.600
And, not only that, as the Chinese have begun
investing in the poorest countries – as

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:07.990
in Africa – many analysts, journalists and
activists have accused Beijing of practicing

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:10.590
a kind of colonialism.

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:17.859
And, beware, this phenomenon is quite widespread:
companies, investors, entrepreneurs, government

00:02:17.859 --> 00:02:19.079
institutions...

00:02:19.079 --> 00:02:20.660
it doesn’t matter...

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:24.829
In recent years the obsession to get money
out of China has been shared, practically,

00:02:24.829 --> 00:02:27.770
by every person in the Chinese economy.

00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:30.220
Surely some of you are thinking...

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:33.010
Chinese people buying all kinds of things
all over the world?

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:35.880
People in Beijing must be rubbing their hands
together...

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:40.829
Well... the truth is that no, nothing could
be further from the truth, in fact throughout

00:02:40.829 --> 00:02:45.980
the last year Xi Jinping’s government has
launched many measures to stop this process...

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:51.040
One moment--the Beijing government wants to
stop this Chinese purchase wave?

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:53.320
Don’t they want to take over the world?

00:02:53.320 --> 00:03:00.330
Well... it's not that they don’t want to...
but they have their reasons.

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:04.730
Listen up.

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:10.630
(THE OTHER FACE OF CHINESE INVESTMENT)

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:13.139
Imagine we want to invest abroad.

00:03:13.139 --> 00:03:15.479
As you can imagine, we can do it in many different
ways:

00:03:15.479 --> 00:03:19.660
For example, we can buy debt from a foreign
government, from a foreign company, from a

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:24.010
foreign bank; we can also buy shares in another
country’s stock market...

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:29.850
But we can also carry out what is known as
Foreign Direct Investment... that is... buy

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:34.350
a local company, open a store, a factory,
a workshop, etc., etcetera.

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:39.940
In other words, invest in a real asset in
the field.

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:44.290
Well, obviously a direct foreign investment...

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:49.660
implies a greater degree of complexity and
greater risk, which is why it’s expected

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:51.410
to generate greater profitability.

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:56.470
Of course, if it weren’t, there would be
no incentive to implement them... after all,

00:03:56.470 --> 00:04:00.410
it’s infinitely easier to buy financial
assets while sitting in front of a computer

00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:03.310
at the office or, even, at home.

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:05.720
Well... the fact is that, paradoxically...

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:10.290
as this type of investment has grown in recent
years, its profitability has plummeted...

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:15.250
According to China's state currency administration,
the rate of return, this type of investment’s

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:20.120
profitability, dropped to barely 0.4% in 2016....

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:25.700
All despite the fact that almost every stock
market was skyrocketing.

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:32.180
Therefore, in spite of the colonialism accusations...

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:36.620
the truth is that many of the Chinese investment
projects are working so badly that they’re

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:45.470
more of a headache than the business of 
the century.

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:46.470
Now, you may be thinking... but Simon...

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:47.470
Where’s the problem?...

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:48.700
Just making too little money?

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:49.960
Well, dear viewers…

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:52.250
according to what we learned on corporate
debt...

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:53.250
Yes, it is.

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:58.590
A large part of this huge international expansion
was financed with debt... debt that, of course,

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:02.290
must be repaid with interest... and if the
investments failed or didn’t generate the

00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:03.720
expected returns...

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:08.720
it could generate a huge solvency problem...
which would obviously seriously affect the

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:13.240
Chinese financial system, which belongs fundamentally
to the government.

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:21.910
Let’s take, for example, the Sinohydro and
China Railway Engineering Corporation case,

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:25.800
two engineering and construction companies
owned by the Chinese government.

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:30.480
See, in 2007, they reached a huge agreement
with the Congolese government.

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:36.230
In exchange for a concession to exploit copper,
they committed to investing 9 billion dollars

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:40.500
in the African country, 3 billion in the mine
and 6 billion in infrastructure.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:45.310
Two government-owned companies that had nothing
to do with mining and were entirely financed

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:47.580
by a Chinese government-owned bank.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:53.030
Well, do you know what has happened since
then?

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:57.710
These Chinese companies have already invested
more than 2 billion dollars and so far everything

00:05:57.710 --> 00:05:58.710
has been a problem...

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:02.390
the price of copper fell, the reserves weren’t
as big as expected, the logistical costs were

00:06:02.390 --> 00:06:03.390
very high...

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:06.370
Because they hadn’t even guaranteed their
power supply...

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:10.000
And of course, all that meant many more costs
and many delays.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:16.110
Only 9 years later, in 2016, they were able
to start extracting copper... in much lower

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:17.590
quantities than expected...

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:21.920
So, whatever the future holds, the reality
is that during the last decade this agreement...

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:27.240
was a big headache... and a great source of
losses.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:31.220
And don’t think that this project was an
exception, the truth is that in recent years,

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:35.550
Chinese companies, government-owned and private
companies, alike, have gotten into serious

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:40.190
debt buying all kinds of assets without caring
about the price.

00:06:40.190 --> 00:06:43.680
But that’s not all.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:46.860
In addition, all these investments mean capital
leaving the country...

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:53.150
at such a high rate that between 2011 and
the beginning of 2017, Chinese currency reserves

00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:55.800
plummeted by a trillion dollars...

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:58.020
And that, my friends, is a lot, a lot of money.

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:01.621
And it’s something that goes in the opposite
direction of what Beijing wants, which in

00:07:01.621 --> 00:07:03.930
recent years tried to strengthen the Yuan.

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:04.930
Now...

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:09.170
Why are Chinese investors so eager to get
money out of the country?

00:07:09.170 --> 00:07:14.330
And, why do they seem so surprisingly bad
when it comes to investing abroad?

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:16.810
Listen up.

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:23.290
(THE BAD INCENTIVES)

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:28.860
Folks, the fact that Chinese investors seem
so incompetent when it comes to investing

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:31.900
abroad is a matter of incentives and bureaucracy.

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:36.950
See... the main priority for the Chinese who
managed to make money, is to transfer part

00:07:36.950 --> 00:07:38.340
of their assets abroad...

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:39.340
Why?

00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:43.639
Well... it’s fundamentally a matter of legal
safety.

00:07:43.639 --> 00:07:50.760
Allow me to explain: As you know China is
a dictatorship... and that means that if tomorrow

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:55.470
you have a problem with political power...
your money could disappear... and not only

00:07:55.470 --> 00:07:57.320
that... as in any dictatorship...

00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:01.530
there are many restrictions... because if
things go wrong, what could be better than

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:04.380
having part of your patrimony protected in
other countries?

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:06.670
Seems sensible, right?

00:08:06.670 --> 00:08:10.210
I’d certainly do it.

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:11.980
Wouldn’t you?.

00:08:11.980 --> 00:08:17.100
Well, that’s one of the explanations for
why so many Chinese businessmen wanted to

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:22.280
invest their money as quickly as possible,
even if that meant paying a premium, a surcharge...

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:24.340
But that isn’t the only reason.

00:08:24.340 --> 00:08:28.560
Additionally, in recent years, since 2008,
the Chinese government promoted easy and cheap

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:30.810
credit as a mechanism to boost the economy...

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:34.430
And, of course, when you can get into debt
under such favorable conditions...

00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:38.419
you won’t be as demanding when selecting
which projects to invest in.

00:08:38.419 --> 00:08:42.270
Yes, this is one of the big problems with
expansive monetary policies.

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:44.650
They encourage bad investments that later...

00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:45.960
are hard to pay off.

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:47.100
But... just a moment...

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:50.900
The entities most responsible for the huge
slab of corporate debt that we’ve talked

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:53.520
about… have been not private companies...

00:08:53.520 --> 00:09:02.700
but state owned ones.

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:08.900
And this... though surprising, also has a
lot to do with incentives…

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:16.020
See, investing outside of the country in large
amounts meant that managers could puff up

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:19.580
their chests before the Beijing government
due to the huge growth that their companies

00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:20.580
had achieved.

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:25.390
On the other hand it meant many of these managers
could move to another country and charge a

00:09:25.390 --> 00:09:26.390
lot of money.

00:09:26.390 --> 00:09:35.370
Let's say that their interests are different
from those of their companies.

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:40.900
And, of course, if business was done in countries
as complicated as Africa...

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:45.490
it was also easier to take a slice, set out
their hand, get a commission.

00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:49.940
In addition, Chinese state-owned companies
have huge bureaucratic structures... which

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:55.010
makes decision-making and adapting to any
changes that may occur very, very slow.

00:09:55.010 --> 00:09:58.160
Yes, state-owned companies tend to be much
more inefficient.

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:06.170
Well... that’s what Xi Jinping wants to
face... while trying to consolidate his power.

00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:13.430
(THE DANGEROUS GAME)

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:18.880
An important point: this huge shopping maelstrom
-which is often done without too much thought-

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:24.110
also happened in Japan in the 80s, that is,
before the great crisis of the early 90s.

00:10:24.110 --> 00:10:27.850
And in Beijing they’re terrified that something
similar will happen to them...

00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:29.420
So, what do you think they did?

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:32.520
Well... they introduced a lot of restrictions
on foreign investments...

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:37.330
in such a way... that now the Chinese government
can veto corporate operations abroad for Chinese

00:10:37.330 --> 00:10:38.330
companies....

00:10:38.330 --> 00:10:41.960
even if they are carried out by private companies.

00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:46.070
And this... has a double intention because
in addition to cooling the foreign investment

00:10:46.070 --> 00:10:50.010
process... it also strongly reinforces Xi
Jinping’s political power.

00:10:50.010 --> 00:10:53.600
Let's say that with this excuse, the Chinese
government has taken an enormous mechanism

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:59.800
out of its sleeve to reward friends and punish
the less loyal.

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:02.210
In addition, with the excuse of increasing
control...

00:11:02.210 --> 00:11:07.270
Beijing is also promoting the merging of government-owned
companies – which until now worked autonomously

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:09.830
– to create huge corporations....

00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:11.810
that give Beijing even more power.

00:11:11.810 --> 00:11:13.529
Of course, all these actions...

00:11:13.529 --> 00:11:18.040
can weigh on the growth of Chinese companies,
decrease competition and increase the bureaucracy

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:22.080
and inefficiency with which the Chinese public
sector operates.

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:30.310
But, yes, Xi Jinping will get even more power.

00:11:30.310 --> 00:11:34.930
China has many strengths, but this is one
of its great weaknesses.

00:11:34.930 --> 00:11:39.480
Even so, Chinese savings are so high... that
it seems difficult to reproduce a financial

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:42.190
crisis like the one Japan experienced...

00:11:42.190 --> 00:11:44.730
but the risk still exists.

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:45.730
Now it’s your turn.

00:11:45.730 --> 00:11:49.600
Do you think that Xi Jinping's ambition will
end up hurting China?

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:52.920
Leave your answer in the comments as well
as the survey.

